Lyme Disease (associated with recent overseas travel)
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Erythema migrans 'bullseye' rash, History of tick bite in endemic region, Migratory large joint arthralgia, Facial nerve palsy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-025
Reason for Visit: Sudden development of severe facial droop, severe headaches, and expanding rash following recent overseas travel, requiring urgent assessment to rule out acute stroke or severe neurological infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient with an acute-onset unilateral facial droop, severe unremitting headaches, and joint pain. Urgent physical assessment is required to immediately rule out an acute cerebrovascular accident (stroke), confirm a peripheral nerve palsy, and evaluate for early neuroborreliosis prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "Half my face has completely paralyzed, my head is splitting, and I have this massive red ring on my leg."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced 2 weeks ago with a low-grade fever, severe fatigue, and generalized systemic malaise shortly after returning from a hiking trip in heavily wooded areas of North America (Connecticut, USA). The patient noted a classic erythema migrans presenting as a slowly expanding target-like 'bullseye' rash on their left thigh at the site of an unrecognized tick bite. Over the last 5 days, the patient developed intense, severe migratory arthralgia predominantly affecting the right knee. Today, the patient woke with a sudden unilateral facial nerve palsy (cranial nerve VII) leading to significant left-sided facial droop. Patient also reports severe, unremitting headaches and a stiff neck indicating mild aseptic meningitis. Denies any severe palpitations or syncope.
Functional Status: Ambulatory but highly distressed by the facial paralysis and severe headache.
Social/Safety Concerns: Patient is terrified they have had a stroke.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 78 | Temp: 37.4 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, marked left-sided facial droop, rubbing neck due to stiffness.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam confirms a lower motor neuron Cranial Nerve VII palsy (Bell's Palsy variant); the patient cannot wrinkle the left forehead, close the left eye tightly, or smile symmetrically. No other focal neurological deficits, ruling out a central stroke. Neck exam reveals mild nuchal rigidity. Skin exam reveals a 15cm classic Erythema Migrans ('bullseye') rash on the left posterior thigh. Cardiac auscultation reveals a regular rhythm with no obvious block. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Erythema migrans 'bullseye' rash, History of tick bite in endemic region, Migratory large joint arthralgia, and Facial nerve palsy.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Lyme Disease (Early Disseminated Phase with Neurological Involvement) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Ischemic Stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The facial palsy involves the forehead (lower motor neuron lesion), indicating a peripheral nerve issue rather than a central stroke, combined with the classic rash.

Idiopathic Bell's Palsy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The concurrent presence of the pathognomonic Erythema Migrans rash, severe migratory arthralgia, and specific endemic travel history.

Cellulitis (at rash site) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinct expanding target-like appearance with central clearing, rather than uniform, hot, tender, swollen erythema.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (High clinical complexity)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an alarming acute facial palsy and severe headache, which naturally raises immediate patient fear of a stroke. The urgent clinical examination was entirely necessary to definitively differentiate a benign peripheral facial nerve palsy (secondary to early disseminated Lyme disease) from a catastrophic acute central stroke. Identifying the pathognomonic Erythema Migrans rash confirmed the diagnosis of neuroborreliosis. Given the neurological involvement, deferring this assessment and delaying targeted antibiotic therapy until the next in-hours period carried a substantial risk of permanent nerve damage or progression to severe carditis (AV block), strictly satisfying the criteria for urgent after-hours review (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribe empiric oral Doxycycline 100mg twice daily for 14-21 days immediately. Provide eye drops (artificial tears) and an eye patch to protect the affected eye from corneal drying/abrasion. Urgent referral for an ECG tomorrow to rule out subclinical atrioventricular heart block, and two-tiered serological testing (EIA followed by Western blot).
Positive Behavioral Change: By taping the affected eye shut strictly at night and using lubricating drops every 2 hours, the patient will prevent severe corneal ulceration; maintaining an intact, pain-free eye will serve as strong positive reinforcement to continue this tedious protective regimen until the nerve function returns.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Medical eye tape, artificial tears.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review in 24-48 hours to perform an ECG and review the progression of the facial palsy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops severe dizziness, unexplained fainting (syncope), profound palpitations, severe shooting neuropathic pain, or if the headache becomes agonizing with intractable vomiting.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is highly confident based on pathognomonic clinical signs and endemic exposure; awaiting serological confirmation.
Clinical Course: The Doxycycline will eradicate the Borrelia bacteria and stop progression. The facial palsy usually completely resolves, but this process may take several weeks to a few months.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The Lyme bacteria can occasionally affect the electrical system of your heart. If you suddenly feel faint, pass out, or feel your heart skipping severe beats, you must call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You do not have a stroke. A tick bite in America transmitted a bacteria that has temporarily paralyzed your facial nerve and caused the distinct rash. The antibiotics will kill the bacteria, but your nerve needs time to slowly heal itself.
Actionable Steps: Start the Doxycycline immediately with food. You must actively protect your open eye from drying out, or you risk permanent vision damage from a scratched cornea.

